---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TopBar from '../components/TopBar.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: 'Mattermore',
      applicationCategory: 'CommunicationApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux, Docker, Kubernetes',
      url: 'https://mattermore.dev/',
      description:
        'A maintained fork of the Mattermost calls plugin that enables group calls on self-hosted Mattermost servers without a paid licence.',
      license: 'https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      offers: { '@type': 'Offer', price: '0', priceCurrency: 'USD' },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: [
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: "Why can't I make group calls on self-hosted Mattermost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'The free edition of Mattermost allows one-to-one audio calls with screen sharing. Group calls are gated behind a Professional or Enterprise licence by a check in the calls plugin, not by any technical limit in the media stack.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Is Mattermore legal to use and distribute?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Mattermore only modifies AGPL-3.0 licensed code and publishes its own changes under the same licence. It does not modify or redistribute any file governed by the Mattermost Source Available Licence.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Will a Mattermost server upgrade remove Mattermore?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'No. Mattermost reinstalls its prepackaged calls plugin only when the prepackaged version is higher than the installed one. Mattermore versions itself well above any upstream release so a server upgrade leaves it in place.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Can I go back to the official Calls plugin?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'Yes. Mattermore keeps the same plugin id as official Calls, so it is a drop-in replacement. Removing it and reinstalling official Calls restores the original behaviour, and your call settings are preserved either way.',
          },
        },
        {
          '@type': 'Question',
          name: 'Does Mattermore work with Mattermost Cloud?',
          acceptedAnswer: {
            '@type': 'Answer',
            text: 'No. Cloud servers do not allow plugin uploads or custom server environment variables. Mattermore is for self-hosted installations only.',
          },
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---
<Base
  title="Mattermore: group calls on self-hosted Mattermost"
  description="Mattermost limits self-hosted servers to one-to-one calls unless you buy Professional. Mattermore is a maintained AGPL build with group calls enabled."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <TopBar />

<main>

<section class="hero">
  <div class="shell">
    <h1>Mattermost, but more.</h1>
    <p class="tagline">Group calls on self-hosted Mattermost, without a Professional licence.</p>

    <ul class="gains">
      <li><b>Group calls in any channel.</b> <span>Stock Mattermost stops you at two people and asks you to upgrade.</span></li>
      <li><b>Nothing else changes.</b> <span>Same plugin, same settings, same call quality. One restriction lifted.</span></li>
      <li><b>Free and open source.</b> <span>AGPL-3.0, built in the open, reversible in two minutes.</span></li>
    </ul>

    <p class="sub">The limit is one boolean in the calls plugin, and Mattermost ships the switch to turn it off. Mattermore is the maintained build that has it flipped, and that keeps up with upstream releases.</p>

    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/install">Install in five minutes</a>
      <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="https://github.com/dennisklappe/mattermore">View the source</a>
    </div>

    <p class="fineprint">Free and AGPL-3.0. Drop-in replacement for the official plugin, reversible in two minutes.</p>

    <div class="switch" id="switch">
      <div class="switch-bar">
        <span class="switch-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="switch-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="switch-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="switch-file">server/enterprise/license.go</span>
      </div>
      <div class="switch-line"><span>MM_CALLS_GROUP_CALLS_ALLOWED=</span><span class="switch-value" id="switch-value">false</span></div>
      <p class="switch-note">Read by <code>GroupCallsAllowed()</code> upstream, in every released version of the plugin.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-soft" id="compare">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head center">
      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <p>Same media stack, same settings, same plugin id. Follow any row for the detail on that feature.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">On a self-hosted server, no paid licence</th>
            <th scope="col">Mattermost free</th>
            <th scope="col">Mattermore</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">One-to-one audio calls</th><td class="yes">Included</td><td class="yes">Included</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Screen sharing</th><td class="yes">Included</td><td class="yes">Included</td></tr>
          <tr class="row-link"><th scope="row"><a href="/group-calls">Group audio calls in any channel</a></th><td class="no">Needs Professional</td><td class="yes">Included</td></tr>
          <tr class="row-link"><th scope="row"><a href="/video-calls">Group video calls</a></th><td class="no">Needs Professional</td><td class="soon">Shipping in v1</td></tr>
          <tr class="row-link"><th scope="row"><a href="/call-recording">Call recording</a></th><td class="no">Needs Enterprise</td><td class="soon">Shipping in v1</td></tr>
          <tr class="row-link"><th scope="row"><a href="/transcription">Transcription, on your own hardware</a></th><td class="no">Needs Enterprise</td><td class="soon">Shipping in v1</td></tr>
          <tr class="row-link"><th scope="row"><a href="/guest-accounts">Guest accounts</a></th><td class="no">Needs Professional</td><td class="soon">Mattermore Server</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="how">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>Nothing here is hidden or clever. You can check each piece against upstream source before you put this near a server people depend on.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="split">
      <div class="split-text">
        <h3>Upstream already ships the switch</h3>
        <p>Group calls are gated by a single function. It checks your licence, and then it checks an environment variable that Mattermost added themselves.</p>
        <p>Mattermore does not patch this file. It carries the Mattermost Source Available Licence rather than the AGPL, and leaving it alone is exactly what keeps this project distributable.</p>
      </div>
      <pre class="snippet"><code><span class="k">func</span> (e *LicenseChecker) GroupCallsAllowed() <span class="k">bool</span> {
    <span class="k">return</span> e.isAtLeastProfessionalLicensed() ||
        os.Getenv(<span class="k">"MM_CALLS_GROUP_CALLS_ALLOWED"</span>) == <span class="k">"true"</span>
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="split flip">
      <div class="split-text">
        <h3>The bundle keeps upgrades from undoing it</h3>
        <p>Calls ships prepackaged with the Mattermost server, and on startup the server reinstalls its own copy over yours whenever the prepackaged version is higher. Set the variable on its own and a routine upgrade can quietly hand you official Calls back.</p>
        <p>Mattermore versions itself above anything Mattermost will ship, so the swap survives.</p>
      </div>
      <pre class="snippet"><code><span class="c"># upstream.env</span>
UPSTREAM_TAG=v1.12.3
MATTERMORE_VERSION=<span class="k">1000.12.3</span></code></pre>
    </div>

    <div class="split">
      <div class="split-text">
        <h3>Upstream fixes arrive by bumping one line</h3>
        <p>The repository holds patches, not a diverged branch. Continuous integration checks out the upstream tag, applies them, and builds. A daily job watches for new upstream releases, checks the patches still apply, and opens an issue.</p>
        <p>A security fix upstream is a one-variable change here, so Mattermore can follow within a day instead of drifting years behind.</p>
      </div>
      <pre class="snippet"><code><span class="c"># pick up a new upstream release</span>
./scripts/check-upstream.sh
./scripts/build.sh</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-soft" id="limits">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>What it will not do for you</h2>
      <p>You are putting this on a server your colleagues depend on, so here is the honest list, before you install rather than after.</p>
    </div>

    <ul class="limits">
      <li>
        <h3>Group video does not work yet</h3>
        <p>Video is limited to direct messages, and that one is a real limit in the upstream interface, which renders exactly one remote participant. Removing a licence check would not produce a working grid. The transport already tags every stream with its sender, so the work is tractable, and it is the next release.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Roughly 50 people per call</h3>
        <p>That is what a single Mattermost server handles. Past it you need the separate rtcd service, which sits behind an Enterprise licence that Mattermore does not touch.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>No recording or transcription</h3>
        <p>These need job service containers that Mattermost distributes and licenses separately. Mattermore changes nothing about them.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>No support contract, no warranty</h3>
        <p>This is a volunteer fork under the AGPL. If group calls are load-bearing for your business, buying Professional is a perfectly good answer, and it funds the upstream work this depends on.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="faq">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="section-head center">
      <h2>Questions</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="faq">
      <details>
        <summary>Why can't I make group calls on self-hosted Mattermost?</summary>
        <p>The free edition gives every server one-to-one audio calls with screen sharing. The moment a third person joins, the plugin refuses. That refusal is a licence check in the calls plugin, not a limitation of the media stack, which is why one boolean is enough to change it.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is this legal to run and to distribute?</summary>
        <p>Mattermore changes AGPL-3.0 code and publishes its changes under the same licence, which is what the AGPL is for. It does not modify or redistribute any file under the Mattermost Source Available Licence, and it enables group calls through an environment variable that upstream ships in every released version.</p>
        <p>Framasoft's <a href="https://framagit.org/framasoft/framateam/mostlymatter">Mostlymatter</a> has taken the same approach with the Mattermost server since 2024.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Will a Mattermost upgrade remove it?</summary>
        <p>No. The server replaces an installed plugin with its prepackaged copy only when the prepackaged version is strictly higher, and Mattermore versions itself above anything upstream ships. If you would rather belt and braces it, set <code>AutomaticPrepackagedPlugins</code> to false.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can I go back to official Calls?</summary>
        <p>Yes, in about two minutes. Mattermore keeps the plugin id <code>com.mattermost.calls</code>, so it is a drop-in replacement. Remove it, reinstall Calls, and your configuration is untouched either way.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Does this work with Mattermost Cloud?</summary>
        <p>No. Cloud servers do not let you upload plugins or set server environment variables. Mattermore is for self-hosted installations only.</p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Am I taking something from Mattermost?</summary>
        <p>Worth asking honestly. Mattermost open-sourced this code under the AGPL and added the environment variable themselves, so neither the licence nor the switch is being worked around. That said, if your organisation can afford Professional, buying it funds the upstream work that makes a fork like this possible at all.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="closer">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>Two steps, five minutes</h2>
    <p>Set one environment variable, upload one plugin bundle. Reversible at every point, and it does not touch your database.</p>
    <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/install">Read the install guide</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

</main>

  <SiteFooter />

  <script is:inline>
    // One moving thing on the page: the switch flips once, shortly after load.
    (function () {
      var el = document.getElementById('switch');
      var value = document.getElementById('switch-value');
      if (!el || !value) return;
      var flip = function () {
        value.textContent = 'true';
        el.classList.add('is-on');
      };
      if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
        flip();
        return;
      }
      window.setTimeout(flip, 900);
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
